tion in the early Christian church."}, {"sourceSlug": "constantius-of-perugia", "sourceName": "Constantius of Perugia", "verb": "DEFINES", "targetSlug": "episcopacy-of-perugia", "targetName": "Episcopacy of Perugia", "context": "Constantius is traditionally considered the first bishop of Perugia, establishing an early episcopal see in the region."}], "places": [{"name": "Perugia, Italy", "role": "Place of Episcopacy and Martyrdom"}, {"name": "Rome, Italy", "role": "Seat of Imperial Authority"}, {"name": "Umbria, Italy", "role": "Region of Activity"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Constantius of Perugia", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "constantius-of-perugia"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q936653", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constantius_of_Perugia"], "tags": ["Religious Figures", "comparative-religion", "doctrine-development", "cultural-diffusion"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Constantius's martyrdom solidified the nascent Christian community in Perugia and contributed to the early Christian tradition of saintly veneration, establishing a regional cult that persisted for centuries.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-15T00:41:27Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29379548490", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Constantius of Perugia (b. 150 CE) was a entrepreneur or business leader of the Classical era, an age of empires, philosophical inquiry, and foundational civilizations, active in Global. Known in the fields of religious figures.", "newValue": "Saint Constantius of Perugia, an early Christian bishop and martyr, served as a foundational figure in the nascent Christian community of Umbria during the mid-2nd century CE, a period marked by intense Roman imperial scrutiny and sporadic persecution. His death around 170 CE, traditionally attributed to the reign of Emperor Marcus Aurelius, cemented his status as a venerated saint, establishing an enduring local cult in Perugia and influencing the hagiographic traditions of the early church. As one of the earliest known bishops of Perugia, his life and martyrdom underscore the precarious existence of Christian leaders seeking to evangelize within the polytheistic Roman Empire.

Constantius's episcopacy in Perugia, then a significant city in the Roman region of Umbria, saw him ministering to a growing but still clandestine Christian population. Historical accounts, though sparse and largely hagiographical, describe his arrest and subsequent torture, including scourging, immersion in boiling water, and ultimately decapitation, often near the city's Forum. These brutal methods were characteristic of Roman efforts to suppress what was perceived as a subversive and atheistic cult, challenging traditional Roman pietas. His steadfast refusal to renounce his faith, even under duress, became a potent symbol of Christian resilience.

The martyrdom of Constantius had a profound impact on the local Christian community, galvanizing believers and providing a powerful example of faith unto death. His burial site outside the Porta San Pietro became a focal point for veneration, leading to the construction of a basilica over his tomb in the early medieval period, which housed his relics. This site became a pilgrimage destination, solidifying Perugia's connection to early Christian history and contributing to the city's spiritual identity. His feast day, celebrated on January 29th, continues to be observed in Perugia, recognizing him as a patron saint."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-15T00:41:27Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29379548490", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "150 CE"}, {"title": "Spiritual seeking and religious community formation", "type": "Idea", "year": "150 CE"}], "newValue": ["The official polytheistic state religion of the Roman Empire viewed monotheistic Christianity with suspicion and hostility.", "The rapid growth of early Christian communities across Roman provinces created social and religious tensions.", "Imperial policies of persecution, particularly under emperors like Marcus Aurelius, targeted Christians for their refusal to worship Roman gods."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-15T00:41:27Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29379548490", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Shaped religious practice and spiritual communities", "type": "Idea", "year": "170 CE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "170 CE"}], "newValue": ["Constantius's martyrdom strengthened the nascent Christian community in Umbria by providing a powerful example of faith.", "His veneration contributed to the developing hagiographic tra